The classical Liouville theorem states that every bounded entire function on the complex plane $ $ is constant. The same conclusion also holds for bounded harmonic functions defined on the whole Euclidean space $ ^n$. As harmonic maps generalize harmonic functions, one of the classical topics in the theory of harmonic maps is the extension of Liouville-type theorems to this setting. One of the main themes of this paper is to establish Liouville-type theorems for several classes of generalized harmonic maps. For this purpose, in this section we recall some classical results in this direction. Although the Liouville-type theorems below are classified according to different assumptions, these results are related to one another and should not be regarded as completely independent.

 {Boundedness of the image}

We first consider the case where the global image $f(M)$ of a harmonic map $f$ is sufficiently ``small'' in $N$, for instance, when $f(M)$ is bounded. Such an assumption often leads to a Liouville-type theorem, and the classical Liouville theorem itself belongs to this category. By means of gradient estimates, Yau extended the classical Liouville theorem to complete Riemannian manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature.

 {theorem}[ {Yau1975Harmonic}]
Let $(M,g)$ be a complete Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature. Then every bounded harmonic function $f$ on $M$ is constant.
 {theorem}

 {remark}
In fact, Yau proved a stronger Liouville-type theorem, for which it is enough to assume that $f$ is bounded from below.
 {remark}

Later, Cheng extended the Liouville theorem to harmonic maps.

 {theorem}[ {Cheng1980Liouville}]
 {ChengLiou}
Let $(M,g)$ be a complete Riemannian manifold with nonnegative Ricci curvature, and let $N$ be a simply connected Riemannian manifold with nonpositive sectional curvature. If $f:M  N$ is a harmonic map whose image is contained in a compact subset of $N$, then $f$ is constant.
 {theorem}

Moreover, Hildebrandt, Jost, and Widman proved the following result.

